T28.01|Property investments include residential, commercial, industrial and specialised assets, held for rent, development or resale. Each has different demand, lease terms, costs and financing. Location and asset quality matter, but investment returns also depend on entry price and capital requirements.~~Owning land, developing a building and operating a leased property are distinct economic activities. Compare income, liquidity and execution risk rather than grouping every property as one asset.|Income property: Earns rent from tenants.;;Development: Creates or substantially improves assets.;;Land investment: Depends largely on future use or resale.|A buyer compares a rented building earning ₹8 lakh NOI with vacant land at the same ₹100 price.~~The building has observable operating income.;;The land requires assumptions about development or resale.;;Compare future investment, timing and permissions.~~Equal purchase prices do not imply equal risk or income.~~No universal appreciation rate is assumed.|Identify the investment model.;;Inspect title and permitted use.;;Model costs, income and exit separately.
T28.02|Property income starts with potential rent, then deducts vacancy, concessions and collection losses to reach effective income. Operating costs maintain the asset's service, while financing, depreciation and capital expenditure require separate treatment. Lease wording determines who bears costs.~~Vacancy can raise costs as well as reduce income. Tenant quality, expiry concentration and rent-free periods affect cash timing.|Potential rent: Income at stated occupancy and pricing.;;Effective rent: After vacancy and concessions.;;Operating costs: Recurring property-level expenses.|Potential annual rent is ₹12 lakh, vacancy loss ₹2 and operating costs ₹3.~~Effective rental income is ₹10.;;NOI is ₹7 before financing and capex.;;Test an additional ₹1 vacancy loss.~~NOI falls to ₹6 if costs remain unchanged.~~Maintenance classification and pass-through charges require consistent definitions.|Analyse lease expiries.;;Separate billing from collection.;;Distinguish operating costs from capex.
T28.03|Net operating income measures property income less defined operating expenses before financing and income tax. A capitalisation rate relates stabilised annual NOI to value. It is a pricing ratio, not a guaranteed total return or a discount rate interchangeable in every model.~~Capitalising unstable income without adjustment can mislead. Growth, required capex, risk and market conditions affect the appropriate valuation.|NOI: Property operating income under a stated definition.;;Cap rate: NOI divided by property value.;;Stabilised NOI: Sustainable income after normalising unusual conditions.|Assume stabilised NOI ₹8 lakh and cap rate 8%.~~Value estimate is ₹8 ÷ 0.08 = ₹100 lakh.;;At 10%, the same NOI implies ₹80.;;Assess why the rate differs and whether income is sustainable.~~Small rate changes can materially alter value.~~The example assumes income and cap-rate conventions match.|Normalise NOI.;;Check comparable transactions.;;Stress cap rates and capital requirements.
T28.04|Property valuation may use comparable sales, income capitalisation, discounted cash flows or replacement-cost analysis. Each method answers a different part of the question and relies on adjustments for location, condition, leases and rights. A price per square foot alone can conceal major differences.~~Use cash-flow projections when lease changes, redevelopment or capex make a single-year income measure inadequate.|Comparable sales: Adjusts prices of similar transactions.;;Income approach: Values expected property cash generation.;;Cost approach: Estimates replacement economics with relevant depreciation.|A property generates ₹8 lakh NOI but needs ₹20 refurbishment immediately; a simple income capitalisation gives ₹100 before that spend.~~Identify capex excluded from NOI.;;Adjust a cash-flow valuation for the immediate ₹20 outflow.;;Avoid comparing the unadjusted ₹100 with a fully refurbished asset.~~Asset condition changes investment value.~~The adjustment also depends on future rent benefits and valuation convention.|Use consistent property rights.;;Include lease and capex schedules.;;Reconcile methods and assumptions.
T28.05|Development feasibility compares land, construction, financing and other costs with expected sales or operating value. The schedule matters because spending usually precedes receipts. Planning, contractor execution and demand uncertainty can change both cost and timing.~~Profit margin on total cost does not measure annualised equity return. Delays can increase interest and prevent refinancing even when eventual sale value is unchanged.|Development cost: Land, construction and supporting expenditure.;;Contingency: Allowance for uncertain overruns.;;Absorption risk: Speed and price of leasing or selling.|Expected sales are ₹150 lakh; land ₹40, construction ₹70 and other costs ₹20.~~Base profit is ₹20.;;A ₹10 overrun cuts it to ₹10.;;Model delayed receipts and added financing costs separately.~~Thin margins can disappear under plausible execution shocks.~~Tax, permissions and sales assumptions require local verification.|Build a dated cost schedule.;;Allow overruns and delays.;;Test sales pace and financing availability.
T28.06|A mortgage is lending secured by property under specified rights. Amortisation divides scheduled payments between interest and principal, with the mix changing as the balance falls. A balloon structure leaves substantial principal for maturity, while floating rates change payments or term under the agreement.~~Affordability requires total cash obligations, not only the initial payment. Collateral value does not replace repayment capacity.|Amortising loan: Principal repaid over time.;;Interest-only loan: Principal largely deferred.;;Floating-rate loan: Interest resets by contract.|A ₹100 lakh loan has 10% annual interest and an illustrative ₹15 annual payment.~~First-year interest is ₹10.;;Principal repayment is ₹5.;;Closing balance is ₹95 before fees or timing effects.~~Only part of the payment reduces debt.~~Monthly amortisation and rate-reset conventions produce different exact schedules.|Reconcile principal and interest.;;Stress rate resets.;;Inspect balloon and prepayment terms.
T28.07|Loan-to-value measures borrowing relative to property value; debt yield compares NOI with debt; debt-service coverage compares defined cash income with required service. They address collateral leverage, income support and payment capacity respectively. Definitions used by lenders can differ.~~A higher appraised value can improve LTV without generating any additional repayment cash. Use all three alongside lease and refinancing analysis.|LTV: Debt divided by property value.;;Debt yield: NOI divided by debt.;;DSCR: Defined cash available divided by debt service.|Property value ₹100 lakh, debt ₹60, NOI ₹9 and annual debt service ₹6.~~LTV is 60%.;;Debt yield is 15%.;;DSCR is 1.5× under the stated definitions.~~Collateral and cash metrics provide complementary information.~~Capex, reserves and taxes may change the lender's coverage measure.|State ratio definitions.;;Stress rent and valuation.;;Review refinancing and reserve requirements.
T28.08|Unlevered property returns measure asset performance before financing; levered returns measure equity cash flows after debt effects. Borrowing can raise equity returns when asset performance exceeds financing costs, but also magnifies losses and introduces payment risk.~~Compare the same asset, period and cost assumptions. A leveraged return excluding debt repayment or necessary capex is incomplete.|Unlevered cash flow: Property cash before debt.;;Levered cash flow: Residual cash after financing.;;Equity return: Distributions and sale proceeds relative to equity.|A property costs ₹100 lakh and generates ₹10 annual cash before financing; debt is ₹60 at 8%, with no principal repayment assumed.~~Interest is ₹4.8.;;Equity cash is ₹5.2 on ₹40 equity, or 13%.;;A ₹20 value decline halves that equity before other changes.~~Leverage magnifies both income yield and price sensitivity.~~This excludes fees, tax and amortisation.|Use dated equity cash flows.;;Include debt repayment.;;Stress income and exit value together.
T28.09|Property joint-venture waterfalls allocate cash among investors and sponsors according to return tiers. Capital return, preferred returns and promoted interests can change payout shares at different outcomes. A promote compensates the sponsor beyond its capital percentage under specified conditions.~~Model the actual sequence and timing; simple ownership percentages are insufficient. Return-of-capital and return-on-capital rules are distinct.|Capital repayment: Returns contributed principal.;;Preferred return: Priority economic return under agreement.;;Promote: Sponsor's enhanced share above defined thresholds.|Assume ₹100 capital, ₹140 proceeds, capital returned first and remaining profit split 80/20 with the sponsor.~~Return ₹100.;;Profit is ₹40.;;Investor receives ₹32 profit; sponsor receives ₹8.~~Distribution rights determine economic participation.~~Real hurdles, catch-ups and interim cash flows require a fuller waterfall.|Read tier order.;;Model dated cash flows.;;Check fees, clawbacks and loss sharing.
T28.10|Corporate finance funds a company whose lenders look to its broader resources; project finance funds a defined project's cash flows and contracts, often through a separate entity. Limited recourse allocates certain risks but does not mean there is no sponsor obligation.~~Project financing relies on contracted revenue, risk allocation, construction completion and a cash waterfall. The legal documents determine who bears each adverse event.|Corporate recourse: Claims against broader company resources.;;Project finance: Repayment centred on project cash flows.;;Limited recourse: Sponsor liability constrained by specified terms.|A sponsor has two projects; one is financed in a separate vehicle with lender claims limited as agreed.~~Trace collateral and contractual guarantees.;;Model repayment from that project's cash.;;Identify completion or support commitments by the sponsor.~~Legal separation and economic independence are different.~~Guarantees can reconnect risks across entities.|Map recourse explicitly.;;Review contract risk allocation.;;Test project cash without assumed sponsor rescue.
T28.11|A special-purpose vehicle holds specified assets and contracts for a project or transaction. It can support clearer ownership and cash-flow allocation, but insolvency remoteness and limited recourse depend on enforceable structure and conduct. Creating a separate company alone is insufficient.~~Related-party transactions, guarantees and cash transfers can undermine intended separation or change who bears risk.|SPV: Entity with a defined purpose.;;Ring-fencing: Restricts assets and cash to specified uses.;;Recourse carve-out: Exception to limited sponsor liability.|An SPV earns ₹15 lakh but owes ₹10 debt service and must retain ₹3 reserves.~~Only ₹2 remains before other costs.;;Check whether distributions are contractually permitted.;;Do not treat all ₹15 as sponsor cash.~~Entity revenue and distributable cash differ.~~Actual protections and restrictions require document review.|Inspect ownership and guarantees.;;Follow the cash waterfall.;;Verify permitted transfers and reserves.
T28.12|Construction risk concerns completion, cost and performance before operation; operating risk concerns availability, maintenance, input costs and service delivery afterward. Passing a risk to a contractor works only if the contract is enforceable and the contractor can perform.~~Completion delay can postpone revenue while interest continues accumulating. Technical capacity and insurance matter alongside contractual penalties.|Completion risk: Asset not ready on time or budget.;;Performance risk: Output below agreed capability.;;Operating risk: Disruptions and higher ongoing costs.|A project expects ₹2 lakh monthly operating cash, but completion is delayed six months and adds ₹3 financing cost.~~Deferred operating cash is ₹12.;;Additional financing cost is ₹3.;;Assess contractor compensation and revised funding needs.~~A delay changes both timing and required capital.~~Deferred revenue is not always permanently lost, so value it by schedule.|Stress cost and schedule.;;Assess contractor capacity.;;Plan maintenance and interruption resilience.
T28.13|Concessions grant rights to provide infrastructure services under defined conditions; offtake contracts specify purchases of output; public-private partnerships allocate responsibilities between public and private parties. These contracts can support predictable revenue but introduce performance, counterparty and policy risks.~~A take-or-pay label does not remove all uncertainty: exceptions, availability requirements and payment enforceability matter.|Concession: Time-limited operating or service rights.;;Offtake: Agreement for output purchase.;;PPP: Public/private allocation of delivery and risk.|A plant has contracted annual revenue ₹20 lakh if availability exceeds an agreed threshold; operating cost is ₹8.~~Base operating cash is ₹12 before other items.;;Model penalties when availability falls.;;Assess the buyer's payment capacity and renewal terms.~~Contracted revenue depends on conditions and counterparty strength.~~The example assumes no universal concession rules.|Read performance conditions.;;Check counterparty credit.;;Model termination, expiry and handback obligations.
T28.14|Debt sizing estimates borrowing consistent with project cash generation, coverage targets and financing terms. Sculpting shapes repayments to expected cash available rather than using equal principal instalments. It reduces mismatch in the base case but cannot eliminate cash-flow risk.~~Use downside coverage, reserves and maturity constraints. Over-optimistic cash forecasts translate directly into excessive debt capacity.|Debt sizing: Chooses sustainable borrowing amount.;;Sculpting: Aligns service with cash-flow profile.;;Coverage constraint: Required margin above debt service.|Assume CFADS ₹12 lakh in a year and target DSCR 1.5×.~~Maximum scheduled debt service is ₹8.;;If interest is ₹3, principal capacity is ₹5.;;Repeat by year and reconcile final balance.~~Repayment capacity follows available cash.~~Present-value sizing also requires rates, dates and lender definitions.|Use consistent CFADS.;;Stress operating forecasts.;;Check maturity and repayment completion.
T28.15|DSCR measures cash coverage of debt service in a period. LLCR compares present value of eligible cash during the loan life with debt outstanding; PLCR uses the project-life cash horizon. Longer project cash flows do not necessarily solve a near-term payment shortage.~~Specify discount rate, eligible cash and dates. Ratios built from different definitions are not directly comparable.|DSCR: Period cash divided by service.;;LLCR: Loan-life cash PV divided by debt.;;PLCR: Project-life cash PV divided by debt.|Debt is ₹50 lakh; loan-life eligible cash PV is ₹65 and project-life PV ₹80; this year's CFADS ₹9 and service ₹10.~~LLCR is 1.3×.;;PLCR is 1.6×.;;Current DSCR is 0.9×.~~Long-run coverage can coexist with immediate shortfall.~~Reserves and restructuring may address timing but need separate analysis.|Match horizons and definitions.;;Inspect weakest annual coverage.;;Avoid substituting lifetime value for liquidity.
T28.16|Project cash waterfalls prioritise payments such as operating costs, taxes, debt service, reserves and distributions. Reserve accounts protect specified obligations and may be unavailable for other uses. Cash in the vehicle is therefore not all freely distributable to shareholders.~~Distribution tests can block payouts even after a profitable year. Track restricted cash separately from available cash.|Operating priority: Funds essential service and costs.;;Debt-service reserve: Supports specified lender payments.;;Distribution lockup: Restricts equity payouts under conditions.|Revenue cash is ₹20 lakh; operations and tax take ₹8, debt service ₹7, required reserve top-up ₹3.~~Cash after operations is ₹12.;;After debt and reserves it is ₹2.;;Check distribution tests before paying equity.~~The waterfall controls cash access.~~Actual order and reserve requirements follow financing documents.|Separate restricted balances.;;Apply payment priorities.;;Test distribution conditions and reserve adequacy.
T28.17|Refinancing replaces or changes existing borrowing and can release equity, lower costs or extend maturity. It can also add leverage, fees and future rollover risk. Equity cash received through new debt is not necessarily operating profit.~~Compare the entire future cash schedule with and without refinancing. High early IRR can reflect accelerated distributions while leaving greater residual risk.|Rate refinancing: Changes financing cost.;;Maturity refinancing: Extends repayment timing.;;Cash-out refinancing: Borrows more to distribute capital.|An asset has ₹40 lakh debt and refinances to ₹60, with ₹2 fees.~~Repay old debt ₹40.;;Net available new cash is ₹18.;;Model interest and repayment on the new ₹60 balance.~~The distribution is financed, not newly earned.~~Higher leverage can worsen downside equity outcomes.|Track source of distributions.;;Include fees and future service.;;Stress valuation and rollover availability.
T28.18|Infrastructure sectors have distinct cash drivers. Energy depends on resource, price and dispatch; transport on traffic and tariffs; utilities on service demand and regulation; social infrastructure on contractual availability and public-service obligations. Sector labels do not imply identical defensiveness.~~Map volume, price, costs, capex and contract risk for the actual asset. Public importance does not guarantee investor repayment.|Energy: Output and pricing or offtake exposure.;;Transport/utilities: Usage, tariffs and operating reliability.;;Social infrastructure: Availability and public-service contract exposure.|A transport asset has 1 lakh trips at ₹100 each and ₹6 lakh operating cost.~~Revenue is ₹10 lakh.;;Operating surplus is ₹4.;;A 20% traffic decline cuts surplus to ₹2 if costs remain fixed.~~Demand sensitivity depends on operating leverage.~~Debt, taxes, capex and concession terms further affect cash.|Use sector-specific drivers.;;Assess regulation and counterparties.;;Stress volume, maintenance and contract expiry.
